This code initializes the pigpio library, sets up I2C communication with the BH1750 sensor, and continuously samples ambient light levels. It activates a relay connected to GPIO23 when lux readings fall below 150 and deactivates it otherwise. The program logs timestamps and lux values to `grow_log.txt`, sampling every 2 seconds. It includes error handling for I2C communication failures and resource cleanup on exit.

To compile and run this code, you'll need to have pigpio installed on your Raspberry Pi. Save the code to a file, say `light_control.c`, and compile it using:

```bash
gcc -o light_control light_control.c -lpigpio
```

Then, run the program:

```bash
./light_control
```

To handle SIGINT (Ctrl+C) and SIGTERM signals for clean termination, you can add signal handling at the beginning of the `main` function:

```c
signal(SIGINT, signal_handler);
signal(SIGTERM, signal_handler);

int signal_handler(int signum) {
    log_entry("Received signal, terminating...");
    gpioTerminate();
    close(i2c_fd);
    return 0;
}
```

This will ensure the program cleans up resources and terminates gracefully when interrupted.

